INFO:self_evolverec:Research reports (2 report(s)):
INFO:self_evolverec:  Report 1: At 0% progress, prioritize a small, testable extension: **a recent-context directed-transition residual for SASRec**. Its benefits remain hypotheses until measured by pinned macro validation HR@10.

**Insights from the starting point.** (1) SASRec already models sequential context; a residual preserves that capability while adding shared transition evidence. This is a smaller initial experiment than replacing its encoder. (2) Users report both unsupported broad associations and stale demand. Conditioning additional evidence on recent interactions directly targets these two failures, although behavioral transitions cannot prove compatibility. (3) Electronics has 14.95% wrong sampled-pair rankings and a −0.4812 lower-decile margin. These motivate better candidate discrimination; embedding similarities near 0.5 provide weaker justification for collapse regularization.

**Insights from related work.** (1) MSMT-LSI separates persistent and recent interests. This motivates conditioning historical evidence on current context rather than uniformly discarding older behavior. (2) Directed graph projection motivates asymmetric behavioral relations. Such relations can distinguish observed next-step patterns from general similarity, without establishing functional complementarity. (3) gSASRec motivates testing negative-sampling objectives. Its findings support a separate loss ablation, not a claim that changing negatives will improve this benchmark.

The resulting directions are temporal relevance, directed behavioral evidence, and ranking supervision:

| Direction | Existing mechanism | Proposed experiment |
|---|---|---|
| Temporal relevance | Long/short-term encoders | Recent-context gate |
| Directed evidence | Interaction graph projection | Smoothed transition residual |
| Ranking supervision | gBCE | Independent multi-negative ablation |

Three candidate ideas follow. Scores use O=originality, P=potential, U=user alignment, M=model alignment, D=implementation difficulty; higher D means harder.

- **Transition residual:** O4/P7/U8/M7/D4. O: useful combination, established components; P: reusable shared evidence, sparse transitions limit coverage; U: targets unsupported associations and stale intent, cannot certify compatibility; M: adds discrimination, margin gains uncertain; D: sparse counts are simple, inference parity requires care.
- **Recent/history representation gate:** O3/P7/U7/M5/D4. O: established design, little novelty; P: adaptable, extra tuning; U: addresses stale and isolated evidence, lacks explicit next-step support; M: may reduce confusion, indirect diagnostic connection; D: small module, requires retraining.
- **Multi-negative gBCE:** O2/P7/U3/M8/D3. O: direct precedent, little novelty; P: broadly applicable, sampling-sensitive; U: improves discrimination potentially, weak connection to specific user failures; M: targets separation, sampled margins may mislead; D: straightforward batching, added compute.

Select the transition residual: it explicitly addresses two user failures and weak positive–negative separation. Build directed counts exclusively from training histories, smooth rare edges, and pool outgoing evidence over recent context. Learn a bounded gate that can suppress unreliable graph evidence. Retain baseline scoring when evidence is absent. Compare against SASRec under identical seeds and pinned validation; report no unmeasured improvement.
INFO:self_evolverec:  Report 2: At 0% progress, prioritize **a recent-context directed-transition residual for SASRec**, revised to control target self-counting. Benefits remain hypotheses until measured by pinned macro validation HR@10.

**Insights from the starting point.** (1) SASRec already models recent dependencies; a residual needs evidence of added value. Preserving its encoder makes this test feasible. (2) Users report unsupported broad associations and stale demand. Recent directed evidence targets both, but cannot establish compatibility or satisfied needs. (3) Electronics has 14.95% wrong sampled-pair rankings and a −0.4812 lower-decile margin. These motivate discrimination experiments; similarities near 0.5 do not establish severe collapse.

**Insights from related work.** (1) MSMT-LSI motivates recent/history conditioning. Older interests may remain relevant when supported by current context. (2) FPMC/FOSSIL already combine preferences and transitions. They reduce the proposal’s originality and provide sparse-data comparators. (3) Directed graph projection and GCL4SR support shared transition evidence. They do not demonstrate compatibility or benchmark gains. (4) gSASRec motivates a separate negative-sampling objective ablation. SpotTarget additionally motivates controlling training-target inclusion before interpreting improved margins.

The directions remain temporal relevance, directed evidence, and ranking supervision, with shortcut control spanning all three:

| Direction | Existing mechanism | Proposed experiment |
|---|---|---|
| Temporal relevance | Long/short-term encoders | Recent-context gate |
| Directed evidence | Graph projection; FPMC/FOSSIL | Smoothed transition residual |
| Ranking supervision | gBCE | Independent multi-negative ablation |
| Shortcut control | Target-link exclusion | User-disjoint graph features |

Scores use O=originality, P=potential, U=user alignment, M=model alignment, D=difficulty; higher D means harder.

- **Transition residual:** O3/P6/U7/M7/D6. O: useful integration, established precedent; P: reusable evidence, sparse coverage limits reach; U: targets unsupported associations and stale intent, cannot certify fit; M: targets confusion, gains remain unmeasured; D: small model change, cross-fitting and scoring parity require care.
- **Recent/history representation gate:** O3/P7/U7/M5/D4. O: useful adaptation, established design; P: extensible, tuning-sensitive; U: targets stale and isolated evidence, lacks direct next-step support; M: may reduce confusion, indirect diagnostic connection; D: small module, requires retraining.
- **Multi-negative gBCE:** O2/P7/U3/M8/D3. O: direct precedent, little novelty; P: broadly useful, sampling-sensitive; U: may improve relevance, weak failure-specific alignment; M: targets separation, margins may mislead; D: simple batching, added compute.

Select the transition residual as an early feasible experiment, explicitly addressing two user failures and weak separation. Cross-fit graph features by training user; smooth, shrink and prune directed counts without renormalizing retained edges. Aggregate recent context and learn a bounded gate initialized near zero. Preserve SASRec when evidence is absent.

Compare SASRec, popularity-only, transition-only, conditional probability versus log-lift, directed versus unordered edges, last-item versus recent pooling, and graph removal; include FPMC/FOSSIL as budget permits. Stage these comparisons within the shared GPU budget. Report category HR@10, coverage, and support/popularity strata alongside macro HR@10. Training margins, simulator responses, and external-paper gains cannot substitute for pinned validation.
