INFO:self_evolverec:The new idea for model in iteration 1:
{
  "description": "Add a recent-context directed-transition residual to each category’s SASRec scores. Training-only transitions provide asymmetric candidate evidence, pooled over recent observed items. A bounded learned gate combines this evidence with SASRec and falls back to SASRec when evidence is absent. Use user-disjoint graph features during training to reduce target self-counting, and evaluate graph-construction distribution shift explicitly.",
  "motivation": "The residual targets unsupported broad associations through observed directed next-step patterns and stale or isolated historical demand through recent context. It targets weak positive–negative separation, especially in Electronics. These are proposed mechanisms, not demonstrated improvements. Behavioral transitions cannot establish compatibility, replacement need, satisfied durable-device demand, or immediate acceptance. SASRec already learns sequential dependencies, and FPMC/FOSSIL establish substantial prior art; therefore incremental value must be demonstrated rather than assumed.",
  "implementation_notes": "Preserve all four category models and routing, preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, DATA_DIR = os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], original splits, and trial-local artifacts. Retain baseline loss, optimizer, and training settings initially; use the existing SASREC_EPOCHS environment value, whose host default is 200. Remain single-GPU and charge every training, diagnostic, debugging, and failed run against the shared 43,200 GPU-second budget. Never access hidden test files or fetch additional datasets or pretrained weights.\n\nDefine an initial reproducible configuration: recent window W=5, exponential position decay beta=0.5, smoothing alpha=10, minimum raw count 2, shrinkage tau=5, maximum outgoing neighbors K=64, log-lift clipping C=3, and five user folds. These are proposed starting settings, not published optima. Assign training users to balanced folds deterministically using a seed-42 permutation of sorted user IDs, separately within each category. For training positions belonging to fold f, construct counts and destination frequencies exclusively from training users outside f. Exclude the entire user contribution before smoothing, thresholding, and pruning. Never count validation interactions. This avoids a user’s own target transitions entering their graph features, although other-user support can still reflect popularity or accidental associations.\n\nFor each graph, count adjacent directed transitions c(i,j); let n_i=sum_j c(i,j). Define q(j)=(d_j+1)/(sum_k d_k+N), where d_j counts destination occurrences in adjacent training transitions and N excludes padding. Let P(j|i)=(c(i,j)+alpha*q(j))/(n_i+alpha). For supported edges, define A(i,j)=[c(i,j)/(c(i,j)+tau)]*clip(log(P(j|i)/q(j)),-C,C). Discard edges with c<2, then retain the K largest absolute A values per source, breaking ties by item ID. Preserve pre-pruning values: do not renormalize outgoing mass. Unstored edges and padding contribute zero; this is an evidence residual, not a normalized probability distribution. Distinguish repeated counts from support by distinct users in diagnostics, since two occurrences from one other user can still be weak evidence.\n\nAt valid prediction position t, extract only sequence positions through t, keeping at most W nonpadding items. Assign normalized weights w_r proportional to exp(-beta*r), where r=0 denotes the most recent item. Compute R(context,j)=sum_r w_r*A(context_r,j). Define context coverage h as the weighted share of context sources with nonempty retained graph rows; this is candidate-independent. Set lambda=2*sigmoid(a^T LayerNorm(features)+b*h+b0), initializing a=b=0 and b0=log(0.01/0.99), so lambda initially equals 0.02. Final scores are the baseline dot products plus lambda*R. Count shrinkage is already inside A; do not apply it twice. With no matching edges the residual is exactly zero. The bounded gate limits amplification but does not itself guarantee robustness or compatibility.\n\nUse a deployment graph formed by an equal-weight average of the five fold graphs, treating missing edges as zero. Serialize these graphs and average their retrieved edge values exactly, without another pruning or normalization step. This retains the same constituent graph estimators used during training, but training uses one user-excluding graph while deployment averages five; the remaining distribution shift must be measured on validation. Compare this default with a full-training graph and with uncorrected training features only as explicitly labeled ablations. A cheaper target-occurrence-subtraction alternative subtracts the supervised transition before all normalization and pruning, but does not remove the user’s other contributions and should not be presented as equivalent to user-level exclusion.\n\nIntroduce one shared scorer score(features, context_ids, candidate_ids, graph_selector) used by training, validation, diagnostics, and exported inference. Flatten valid B×L positions and pass their matching recent contexts; support both position-specific candidate arrays and final-position candidate arrays explicitly. Store sorted nonzero edge keys i*(N+1)+j with weights as registered model buffers, using vectorized searchsorted and equality checks; chunk lookups to bound memory. Do not assume sparse.sampled_addmm provides arbitrary transition lookup. Context plumbing is the main reason the difficulty rating increases to 6.\n\nRetain view.model as the actual trained nn.Module, view.item_emb, user_train, meta_data, review_data, and the existing log2feats(sequence)/predict_score(features, items) compatibility interface. Provide an optional explicit context argument for predict_score and use it throughout new code. For legacy two-argument calls, the compatibility view must bind context during the immediately preceding log2feats call, validate matching feature shape and identity, and reject missing or stale bindings rather than silently return baseline-only scores. The scorer itself must remain explicit and stateless. Update DiagnosisProbe to pass context directly, including fold selectors for training diagnostics. Training-loader batches remain (None, sequence, positive, negative), with B×L tensors.\n\nRetain DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), and diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis). Return measured metrics and metric_definitions; include the existing embedding and positive–negative margin statistics plus transition coverage, absolute residual magnitude, and margins stratified by singleton versus multiple-user transition support. Distinguish raw embedding dot-product margins from complete-scoring margins. Embedding similarity near 0.5 is insufficient evidence of severe collapse, and unavailable category metadata must produce an explicitly unavailable breakdown rather than fabricated categories. The interpreter prompt must include supplied measurements, definitions, and missing-data limitations. Review ratings/text and item ratings/categories/prices/stores are unavailable despite generic schema examples; only public histories, titles, descriptions, and timestamps may be used. Simulator diversity/conformity traits remain unobserved. Keep uncertain recommendations separate from explicit mismatches.\n\nExport model parameters, graph buffers, gate parameters, item mapping, configuration, and shared scoring implementation beneath the trial directory. Update the predictor writer so working/predict.py loads the complete trained architecture and preserves category routing. For full-catalog recommendations to the twenty real training users, score items in chunks, add identical transition evidence, mask padding and observed items under the established recommendation contract, and merge chunk top-k results. Preserve the host evaluator’s candidate and masking rules unchanged. Verify that arbitrary candidate scores equal the corresponding full-catalog score entries, that cross-fitting excludes each training user’s contributions, and that saved/reloaded scores agree.\n\nBegin with matched SASRec and the specified cross-fitted residual. Reserve budget before further ablations: popularity-only, transition-only, graph removal, conditional probability versus log-lift, adjacent transitions versus unordered co-occurrence, last-item versus recent pooling, and FPMC/FOSSIL. For the conditional-probability variant, replace supported-edge A values by P using the same support and pruning conventions; label this a truncated residual. For unordered co-occurrence, symmetrize the same adjacent pairs rather than silently changing to all-history pairs. Keep multi-negative gBCE separate initially to isolate architectural effects. Use only pinned macro validation HR@10 for selection, retaining the exact 200 validation users/category, 99 unique negatives, and evaluation seed 42. Report all four category scores, graph coverage, and validation strata by target popularity and transition support as supporting diagnostics. Do not infer validation improvement from training margins. Limit adaptive tuning and record every tried configuration because repeated selection on the same 800 validation users can overfit. No configuration has yet demonstrated a benchmark gain.",
