INFO:self_evolverec:Research reports (2 report(s)):
INFO:self_evolverec:  Report 1: SASRec offers three starting insights: causal attention represents changing interests, but the diagnosis suggests stale evidence still influences recommendations; dot-product ranking captures associations without establishing practical compatibility; sampled BCE can separate easy pairs while leaving difficult rankings unresolved. Electronics exhibits the clearest measured weakness: 14.95% wrong pairwise rankings and a −0.4812 tenth-percentile margin. Embedding similarity near 0.5 does not justify prioritizing collapse remedies.

Related work suggests three complementary directions. TGCL4SR combines global temporal transitions with local sequence encoding, motivating cross-user evidence when individual histories are sparse. LS4SRec separates long- and short-term interests, motivating recency-dependent aggregation. gSASRec addresses sampled-BCE overconfidence, motivating an objective ablation; none establishes improvements on this benchmark.

These insights define three research directions: collaborative evidence, temporal relevance, and ranking calibration. Their common framework is:

| Component | Evidence | Intended contribution | Unresolved limitation |
|---|---|---|---|
| SASRec | Ordered personal history | Personalized sequence representation | Unsupported associations |
| Directed transitions | Training-user consecutive interactions | Evidence for plausible next steps | Association is not compatibility |
| Co-occurrence | Shared training histories | Repeated specific interests | Popularity and stale interests |
| Recency weighting | Relative interaction position | Current-interest emphasis | Position is an imperfect clock |
| Loss calibration | Sampled positive–negative scores | Difficult-case separation | Unobserved items may be relevant |

Three atomic candidates follow. Scores use O/F/U/M/D for originality, future potential, user alignment, model alignment, and implementation difficulty; higher D means harder.

| Candidate | O/F/U/M/D | Positive assessment | Negative assessment |
|---|---|---|---|
| Recent transition residual | 4/7/8/7/3 | Simple collaborative evidence targets unsupported broad matches and stale interests | Cannot establish equipment compatibility or satisfied needs |
| Learned recent/history gate | 4/7/8/6/5 | Directly adjusts temporal emphasis | Sparse histories may destabilize routing |
| Warm-start multi-negative loss | 3/7/4/8/4 | Directly targets weak separation | Does not explicitly resolve two user-facing failures |

Across candidates, originality comes from adaptation rather than a new model family; future potential depends on measurable complementarity. User alignment is strongest for temporal evidence, whereas model alignment is strongest for loss changes. Difficulty favors a sparse residual over additional trained branches.

Select **recent transition residual** at 0% progress. It explicitly targets broad unsupported recommendations through observed next-step associations and older isolated interests through recent-history weighting. Its model-facing target is Electronics’ lower-tail ranking confusion. These are hypotheses, not demonstrated improvements.

Build directed transition and normalized co-occurrence tables exclusively from training histories. Aggregate their scores over recent items with exponential decay, then blend separately normalized branch scores with SASRec. Compare each branch alone and the combined model using pinned four-category macro validation HR@10. Include zero blend weight to recover the baseline. Freeze normalization before evaluation; never normalize against the evaluator’s candidate list. Retain full-catalog simulator diagnostics separately, since sampled HR@10 does not establish full-catalog quality.
INFO:self_evolverec:  Report 2: SASRec offers three starting insights: causal attention can represent changing interests, yet stale evidence remains a diagnosed concern; dot products encode associations without establishing compatibility; sampled BCE can separate easy pairs while difficult rankings remain unresolved. Electronics has 14.95% wrong pairwise rankings and a −0.4812 tenth-percentile margin. Embedding similarity near 0.5 provides little evidence for prioritizing collapse remedies.

Related work provides three directions: TGCL4SR motivates global/local collaborative evidence; LS4SRec motivates separating recent and persistent interests; gSASRec motivates testing sampling and calibration together. These works establish neither gains here nor that collaborative transitions identify practical fit.

| Component | Evidence | Intended contribution | Unresolved limitation |
|---|---|---|---|
| SASRec | Ordered personal history | Personalized sequence representation | Unsupported associations |
| Directed transitions | Consecutive training interactions | Plausible next-step evidence | Association is not compatibility |
| Co-occurrence | Binary training-user incidence | Repeated collaborative support | Popularity and rare coincidences |
| Recency weighting | Relative interaction position | Current-interest emphasis | Position is an imperfect clock |
| Loss calibration | Sampled scores | Difficult-case separation | Unobserved items may be relevant |

Three atomic candidates remain. O/F/U/M/D denote originality, future potential, user alignment, model alignment, and implementation difficulty; higher D means harder.

| Candidate | O/F/U/M/D | Positive assessment | Negative assessment |
|---|---|---|---|
| Recent transition residual | 3/6/7/7/5 | Tests collaborative support and recency with frozen SASRec | Established ensemble; graph diagnostics add complexity |
| Learned recent/history gate | 4/7/8/6/5 | Direct temporal adaptation | Sparse routing evidence; added optimization |
| Warm-start multi-negative loss | 3/7/4/8/4 | Targets weak separation | Does not explicitly address two user failures |

Select **recent transition residual** at 0% progress, with reduced novelty/potential ratings and increased implementation difficulty. It targets unsupported broad matches through observed next-step support and stale isolated interests through recent weighting. Its model-facing target is lower-tail ranking confusion, particularly Electronics. Compatibility, satisfied needs, and immediate acceptance remain unobserved; improvements are hypotheses.

Construct training-only directed counts and cosine ItemKNN, normalize before source-row top-K, and freeze training-derived score scales. Blend recent-history graph scores with SASRec using a tiny shared grid containing zero. ItemKNN and sequential-rule references clarify that this is an established neighborhood ensemble, not a new graph architecture.

Preserve identical pinned validation candidates. Ablate branches and popularity; report coverage, rescues/regressions, category deltas, history/popularity strata, and paired bootstrap uncertainty. Record every inspected configuration: selection intervals remain optimistic. Compare matched-negative BCE/gBCE when budget permits, then test one preregistered ensemble on the stronger comparator.

Diagnostics must score each causal prefix through the complete hybrid. Report ordinary training-graph margins alongside graph-only user-exclusion margins where user identity is recoverable; target inclusion can inflate training fit. Exclusion removes all user contributions and recomputes normalization/pruning, while neural parameters remain unchanged. The anonymous loader cannot silently supply user identity. Use pinned macro validation HR@10 for selection; training margins and sampled rankings do not establish full-catalog quality.
