{
  "description": "Add a sparse, recency-weighted collaborative residual to each category’s SASRec scores. Directed transitions supply observed next-step associations, and cosine ItemKNN supplies collaborative support. Select a small shared blend configuration using pinned four-category macro validation HR@10. Treat the method as a neighborhood/transition ensemble with limited architectural novelty.",
  "motivation": "The user diagnosis identifies unsupported broad associations and excessive influence from older or isolated interactions. Recent collaborative evidence explicitly targets both patterns, although it can also reproduce them and must be validated. The quantitative target is weak lower-tail positive–negative separation, most pronounced in Electronics. Implicit interactions cannot establish equipment compatibility, replacement need, satisfied demand, or simulator traits. Scores near 0.5 on the mapped embedding-similarity diagnostic do not establish severe collapse.",
  "implementation_notes": "Preserve all four categories and dataset routing, preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, DATA_DIR=os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], the original splits, and the SASREC_EPOCHS environment default of 200. Write checkpoints, sparse tables, score scales, configuration manifests, and the predictor beneath the trial directory. Do not inspect hidden tests, obtain pretrained weights, use excluded metadata/reviews, or alter the evaluator. All training and diagnostics count toward the shared 43,200 GPU-second budget; operate on one GPU.\n\nFor each category, retain chronological training histories. Build binary deduplicated user–item incidence X, item supports d, and C=X.T@X. Define cosine similarities C_ij/sqrt(d_i*d_j), clear the diagonal, and retain the largest K=100 similarities per source row AFTER normalization. Do not renormalize cosine rows after pruning. Directed counts include every adjacent chronological training event, including repeated events; explicitly remove self-transitions. Divide each transition count by its source's total remaining outgoing count BEFORE retaining source-row top-100 entries; do not renormalize afterward. A source without outgoing mass contributes zero. Pruning can break cosine symmetry. Use bounded sparse blocks for co-occurrence products and monitor intermediate nonzeros; never materialize a dense catalog-square matrix. Fix block sizing from memory constraints rather than validation accuracy.\n\nAt inference, use the last W=10 nonpadding interactions, weighting positions by exp(-age/3) and normalizing weights to sum to one. Preserve repeated source occurrences. Empty histories receive zero graph scores. Aggregate outgoing transition and cosine rows for arbitrary candidate IDs. Fit a separate standard deviation for each scoring branch and category from at most 10,000 training prefixes, sampled with a dedicated seed-42 generator, each paired with 32 uniformly sampled items excluding that user's training items. Bound each standard deviation below by 1e-6 and divide scores by it; omit centering because candidate-independent offsets do not change rankings. Freeze these scales before validation and reuse them for sampled and full-catalog inference. Do not normalize over evaluator candidates. If a branch is identically zero, disable it and record that fact.\n\nPrespecify the shared grid (transition weight, co-occurrence weight)={(0,0),(0.1,0),(0,0.1),(0.1,0.1),(0.3,0),(0,0.3),(0.3,0.3)}. Use one shared pair across all four datasets, with category-specific fixed scales. Freeze the original baseline checkpoint-selection rule and training settings before comparisons; document that rule exactly in the manifest and do not tune it alongside blend weights. If the baseline has no checkpoint-selection rule, use the final configured epoch. Break equal macro HR@10 by smaller total blend weight, then lexicographic order. Zero weights preserve SASRec rankings. Report every attempted configuration and its GPU seconds, including failures.\n\nCache validation branch scores only for the identical host-authorized pinned users/candidates; never regenerate negatives or replace the host evaluator. Compute per-user top-10 hits with its tie handling. Compare branch-only scores, transition-only and co-occurrence-only residuals, and the combined model. Report both-hit, SASRec-only-hit, branch-only-hit, neither-hit, ensemble rescues/regressions, nonzero target/candidate coverage, and HR@10 conditional on target coverage with denominators. Stratify descriptively using training-only item-popularity quartiles and history-length bins. A log1p(training interaction count) popularity-only ranking is a diagnostic control, not another blend parameter. These inspections do not authorize additional weight searches.\n\nFor uncertainty, use a dedicated seed-42 generator for 2,000 paired bootstrap replicates, independently resampling 200 users with replacement within each dataset. Reuse each sampled user's outcomes for both models, average the four dataset hit-rate differences, and report the 2.5th/97.5th percentiles and category deltas. These intervals condition on trained models and fixed candidates and do not correct winner-selection optimism. Full-catalog simulator results for twenty real training users remain separate qualitative evidence; uncertain recommendations are not demonstrated dislikes.\n\nAs a budget-dependent follow-up, compare baseline single-negative BCE, BCE with 16 uniform negatives, and gBCE with the same 16 negatives, architecture, seeds, epochs, optimizer, and batch settings. Mask known training positives, never held-out labels. Fix the gBCE calibration parameter and its exact loss definition from the cited implementation before execution; record them, rather than silently treating gBCE as ordinary BCE. Run comparisons in that fixed order only when a measured runtime estimate fits the remaining budget. Then test the already selected residual configuration once on the strongest neural comparator; record this additional validation inspection. A hard-negative curriculum is a later hypothesis, not part of this initial residual experiment.\n\nUpdate the predictor writer so working/predict.py loads identical category models, sparse tables, scales, recency parameters, and weights. Ensure candidate scores agree between batched candidate queries and full-catalog chunks. Expose the actual combined nn.Module as view.model, with frozen graph buffers or a module-owned sparse scorer. Preserve view.item_emb, log2feats(sequence), predict_score(features, items), user_train, meta_data, and review_data. Because the legacy feature-only scoring signature does not contain graph context, retain it for the neural compatibility path and add an explicit hybrid scoring method accepting both hidden features and their sequence prefixes. Adapt diagnostics to invoke that method; do not silently report neural-only margins as hybrid margins.\n\nRetain DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), and diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis). The loader remains (None, sequence, positive, negative), each tensor B×L. In eval mode without gradients, position t must use h_t and sequence[:t+1] for both target scores; mask padding and invalid positive/negative IDs. Report valid-pair count, mean positive/negative scores, mean and tenth-percentile margin, fraction margin<0, fraction margin<0.5, and pairwise accuracy defined as P(margin>0)+0.5P(margin=0). Define every output explicitly. Label embedding similarity as a neural-item-embedding diagnostic, and mark unavailable category breakdowns as unavailable rather than inventing categories.\n\nOrdinary training-graph diagnostics can benefit from the diagnosed user's own target contributions. For an identifiable user, construct an additional graph-only exclusion view by subtracting ALL of that user's binary co-occurrence/support contributions and ALL chronological transition events, then recomputing affected cosine similarities, transition denominators, and top-K from unpruned counts. Rebuilding from retained neighbors alone is insufficient because excluded neighbors can enter top-K. Keep neural parameters, prefixes, candidate pairs, blend weights, and frozen score scales identical; the scales are explicitly not leave-one-user-out. This measures dependence on self-contributed graph associations, not full user exclusion or generalization. The anonymous loader requires an exact, unique match against known training sequence construction, or a separate identity-indexed diagnostic pass over public training histories. If identity cannot be recovered unambiguously or recomputation exceeds budget, report exclusion diagnostics as unavailable with the reason and evaluated-user count. Never guess identities or compare unmatched pairs. The interpreter prompt must use measured metrics and definitions and disclose training-target inclusion, exclusions, unavailable fields, and sampling limitations.",
  "pseudocode": "set_preprocessing_seed(0)\nset_training_seed(42)\nrecord_fixed_training_checkpoint_rule_and_shared_weight_grid()\n\nfor category in FOUR_CATEGORIES:\n    neural = train_original_sasrec(category, configured_epochs_default=200)\n    histories = chronological_training_histories(category)\n    X = binary_deduplicated_user_item_incidence(histories)\n    support = column_sum(X)\n    co_counts = blockwise_sparse_product(X.T, X)\n    cosine = co_counts / sqrt_outer_support_on_nonzeros(support)\n    cosine = clear_diagonal(cosine)\n    cosine = source_row_top_k(cosine, k=100)  # normalize before pruning\n\n    transition_counts = adjacent_event_counts(histories, remove_self=True)\n    transition = divide_by_full_source_row_sums(transition_counts)\n    transition = source_row_top_k(transition, k=100)  # no post-prune renormalization\n    scales = fit_training_only_branch_std(\n        neural, transition, cosine,\n        max_prefixes=10000, negatives_per_prefix=32, seed=42)\n    save_category_artifacts_under_trial_directory()\n\n    def hybrid_score(prefix, items, weights, hidden=None, graphs=None):\n        recent = last_nonpadding_items(prefix, 10)\n        recency = normalized_exp_weights(recent, decay_length=3)\n        neural_score = neural.score(prefix, items, hidden=hidden)\n        transition_score = weighted_outgoing_lookup(graphs.transition, recent, items, recency)\n        cosine_score = weighted_outgoing_lookup(graphs.cosine, recent, items, recency)\n        return (neural_score / scales.neural\n                + weights.transition * transition_score / scales.transition\n                + weights.cosine * cosine_score / scales.cosine)\n\ncache_scores_only_on_host_authorized_identical_pinned_candidates()\nweights = select_shared_grid_by_four_category_macro_hr10(\n    tie_break='minimum total weight, then lexicographic')\nreport_branch_ablations_coverage_rescues_regressions_and_all_inspected_settings()\npaired_stratified_bootstrap(replicates=2000, seed=42)\n\nwith evaluation_mode_and_no_grad():\n    for _, sequence, positive, negative in train_loader:\n        hidden = view.log2feats(sequence)\n        for t in valid_target_positions(sequence, positive, negative):\n            prefix = sequence[:, :t+1]\n            pos_score = hybrid_score(prefix, positive[:, t], weights, hidden[:, t])\n            neg_score = hybrid_score(prefix, negative[:, t], weights, hidden[:, t])\n            accumulate_defined_pair_metrics(pos_score, neg_score)\n            if user_identity_is_unambiguous_and_exclusion_fits_budget():\n                excluded_graphs = rebuild_from_counts_without_all_user_contributions(user)\n                score_identical_prefix_and_pair_with(excluded_graphs, fixed_scales=True)\n            else:\n                record_exclusion_unavailability()\n\nwrite_predictor_with_identical_architecture_routing_and_hybrid_scoring()\nverify_candidate_scores_match_full_catalog_chunk_scores()\nreturn_diagnosis_metrics_and_metric_definitions()",
