  "implementation_notes": "Retain DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), and diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis), returning metrics and metric_definitions. Use view.model as the actual trained module; preserve view.item_emb, log2feats, predict_score, user_train, meta_data, and review_data compatibility. Restore every submodule's original training flag after probing, including on exceptions. Use no_grad, fixed weights, evaluation mode, and bounded batches. Preserve existing pairwise and embedding measurements, excluding padding. Explain that the mapped mean cosine is not a validated collapse measure, approximately 0.5 means mean cosine zero, and raw margins depend on score scale. Record counts, item norms, and pairwise ordering rates; if adding effective rank, define its entropy using normalized squared singular values. Keep unavailable metadata-category lists empty and return null plus reasons for absent measurements.\n\nPreserve DATA_DIR = os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], all four dataset routes, existing splits, preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, and the environment-controlled SASREC_EPOCHS default of 200. Write checkpoints, caches, manifests, and generated predictors beneath the trial directory. Do not inspect hidden tests, obtain external data or pretrained weights, or change the host evaluator. Available content is limited to public titles, descriptions, timestamps, and interaction histories. Ignore the generic input schema's unavailable ratings, review text, prices, stores, and item categories. Simulator traits, ownership, satisfied needs, prerequisites, and compatibility remain unknown unless directly supported by available evidence.\n\nFreeze a manifest before diagnostic outcomes: per category, deterministically sample up to 200 eligible training users using a dedicated seed-42 RNG and sorted user IDs; record actual counts and IDs. Use the last 50 visible events for this diagnostic window, k=1 recent-event removal, five shuffle draws with recorded seeds, and 1,000 user-bootstrap replicates. These are proposed fixed settings, not SASRec standards. For reproducible rank probes, take each selected user's final training interaction as a pseudo-target and the preceding training prefix as context; this is in-sample sensitivity evidence, not validation. Require at least two context events for paired recent/older removal. Exclude pseudo-targets already in the visible prefix from target-rank comparisons and report coverage. Sample up to 999 unique category-local training items excluding the original visible prefix and pseudo-target, then add the pseudo-target. Keep each user's candidates and original-history exclusion mask identical across variants so removed items cannot enter the candidate pool. Record pools and RNG state; these diagnostic pools do not replace the pinned benchmark's 99 negatives. Retain separate full-catalog unseen-item recommendation lists for the prescribed twenty real training users, five per category, using their available training histories and chunked scoring. Without relevance labels, those lists support descriptive coverage, concentration, and stability only.\n\nCompute popularity counts and first-observed item timestamps exclusively from training interactions, separately per category. Fit L2-normalized title TF–IDF on available training-item titles with explicit settings: lowercase, word unigrams and bigrams, min_df=1, sublinear_tf=True, smooth_idf=True, and the scikit-learn default word token pattern. Save the vocabulary, IDF values, package version, and title-availability counts. Never replace missing titles with invented content. Match k recent events to k distinct older events from the same visible prefix. Require absolute log1p-popularity difference <=0.25 and title cosine >=0.5; exclude missing or empty titles. Find maximum-cardinality, minimum-cost matching with cost popularity_difference/0.25 + (1-cosine)/0.5 and deterministic position-based tie-breaking. Use only complete matches for paired comparisons, report coverage, and retain unmatched recent-removal measurements separately. Do not relax thresholds silently. If sensitivity analysis is run, preregister the alternative pairs (0.125, 0.75) and (0.5, 0.25), count their cost, and report all results without selecting a favorable threshold.\n\nApply identical operators to matched recent and older events. Compaction removes events and right-aligns survivors. Position-preserving content ablation zeros only the selected item's embedding contribution, retaining original position IDs, attention-validity pattern, tensor length, and readout index. Position-preserving attention exclusion masks selected events as keys and values in every attention layer while preserving survivor positions and the original readout slot; document that a retained query/residual can still carry information. Handle fully masked rows explicitly and report invalid cases rather than allowing NaNs. Do not implement either operator by substituting padding IDs. These controls require architecture-specific forward support; when unavailable, report that limitation instead of approximating it through the compatibility interface. Their differences are not a pure additive positional effect. For left-padded SASRec, baseline readout is the final valid position; preserve it in fixed-position controls and use the final survivor after compaction.\n\nFor constrained shuffling, compute minimum training timestamps per item. Preserve the existing split and retained-window item multiset: shuffle after truncation, leave padding and timestamp slots fixed, and use the input event order as a stable secondary key for equal timestamps. Freeze every position whose original timestamp equals that item's first observed timestamp, including ties. Traverse positions forward; among nonfrozen j>=i, including i, uniformly select positions for which both swapped items are available at their destination timestamps, allowing equality. Use a dedicated seeded RNG. First appearance is observed availability, not verified release date. Return unavailable when timestamps cannot be parsed or aligned reliably. Include full unconstrained shuffles and older-prefix shuffles retaining the latest event as separate controls. Record unchanged-history rate, changed-position fraction, frozen fraction, and distinct outputs across five draws. For windows of at most seven events, enumerate unique permutations to compare availability-feasible support with support reachable under the frozen-position sequential sampler; report larger windows as unchecked. Do not call this sampler uniform over all feasible permutations.\n\nRank with one-based positions and deterministic item-ID tie-breaking. Define target displacement as perturbed rank minus original rank, so positive means deterioration. Report target displacement, diagnostic target-hit change, recent-minus-matched-older differences, mean absolute displacement across fixed candidates, and top-10 intersection size divided by min(10, candidate count). Aggregate draws within users before bootstrapping users, separately by category. Training-target hit changes are in-sample diagnostics and cannot establish counterfactual preference. For host validation comparisons, retain exactly 200 users per category, 99 unique negatives, and evaluation seed 42. Where the host exposes paired user hits, bootstrap within categories and recompute equal-weight macro differences; otherwise report aggregate metrics without fabricating intervals. One changed hit changes macro HR@10 by 0.00125. Intervals condition on users, fixed candidates, and training seed; they do not cover candidate randomness, training variability, or adaptive selection.\n\nBefore validation outcomes, register at most three model hypotheses: reduced positional information, shorter context, and history dropout or multi-positive training. Treat probe-to-ablation mappings as hypotheses, with no unsupported universal sensitivity threshold. A concrete small comparison uses the same three interventions for both search policies: reduced positional contribution by factor 0.5, context cap reduced to max(2, floor(baseline_cap/2)), and training history dropout probability 0.1. Define the guided policy to choose the intervention associated with the largest affected-user fraction: changed top-10 set under constrained shuffling for positional reduction, unchanged top-10 set after older-prefix exclusion for shorter context, and worsened pseudo-target rank after recent content ablation for dropout. Resolve ties in that listed order; use only available probes and record missing branches. These decision rules are proposed operational choices, not established prescriptions. The unguided policy selects one intervention uniformly with a dedicated seed-42 RNG. Allocate each policy the same total GPU-second cap, including its diagnostics; cache identical trials and charge shared cost symmetrically. Use one baseline plus at most three distinct intervention trials, one fixed final checkpoint per trial, and no validation-driven early stopping. Set and log per-trial wall-time caps before launch so all training, diagnostics, debugging, and failures remain within 43,200 GPU-seconds. This small comparison is descriptive evidence, not a strong demonstration of diagnostic usefulness. Log every comparison and failure. Evaluate clean-history pinned validation HR@10 separately from robustness. Title and popularity matching may leave residual confounding; content matching and guided search can favor shortcuts, so report popularity/history-length strata and each category's change. Any future inference architecture change must update the predictor writer so working/predict.py loads the trained model exactly. The diagnostic extension alone requires no deployed inference change.",
