  "motivation": "Existing embedding and training-margin probes cannot establish whether SASRec uses recent, repeated, specific evidence effectively. At 0% research progress, diagnostics are a feasible first step, but they must acknowledge positional confounding, in-sample targets, unrealistic perturbations, and the absence of observed current intent. A recent-history residual remains a subsequent training hypothesis rather than an assumed improvement.",
  "implementation_notes": "Preserve DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), and diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis). Use view.model as the actual module. Retain embedding alignment and sampled-margin probes, explicitly documenting their limited interpretation and reporting valid counts; unavailable category metadata must produce an unavailable status rather than invented category breakdowns. Return metrics and metric_definitions, with null values and missing-data reasons instead of zero-valued empty summaries.\n\nSpecify a deterministic diagnostic configuration: seed 42; at most 50 eligible users per category selected using a dedicated generator; one latest eligible training-prefix target per user; at least eight nonpadding prefix items after truncation to the model's sequence limit. Use r=min(3,floor((n-1)/3)) removals. The recent pool contains the newest 2r positions before the final item; the older pool contains the earliest 2r positions; the random pool contains all nonfinal positions. Derive item-frequency tertiles from category training counts, with item ID breaking frequency ties. Enumerate recent/older r-position subsets and select a pair minimizing L1 distance between their tertile-count vectors, breaking ties lexicographically. Select a random r-subset with the same vector when feasible using the dedicated generator; otherwise minimize the same discrepancy and record it. Report realized positions, removed-frequency distributions, and matching discrepancies. Handle last-item removal separately and do not interpret it as an equal-count temporal comparison.\n\nFor each case, cache the target plus up to 99 unique negatives sampled without replacement from its category, excluding padding, the target, and the user's complete original training history. If fewer negatives exist, use all eligible items and report the candidate count. These auxiliary candidates are separate from the host's evaluator: matching seed 42 does not reproduce its sampling implementation. Freeze candidate IDs and original seen-item exclusions across all perturbations, including catalog comparisons. Establish a baseline eligible catalog by excluding all original training-history items except the diagnostic target; targets also appearing in the prefix must be flagged and reported separately.\n\nOrdinary removals compact survivors and restore baseline right-aligned padding. For the position-preserving control, retain original position IDs, mask removed positions as attention keys/values at every encoder layer, and suppress their query outputs. Keep the final item intact. Zeroing an embedding alone is insufficient. Implement this through an optional architecture-specific diagnostic hook; the baseline log2feats(sequence) contract alone does not guarantee support. If unsupported, return an explicit missing-control reason and restrict interpretation. This control still changes attention context and does not isolate user-preference causality.\n\nFor local shuffling, permute the last min(5,n-1) nonfinal items within their original occupied positions while retaining the final item and item multiset. Use a deterministic nonidentity permutation. Check item-availability constraints only when usable public timestamps exist; otherwise label plausibility unverified. Repeated-interest slices may use exact repeats or an explicitly specified title/description similarity proxy; do not infer ownership, fulfilled demand, compatibility, dislike, or simulator traits.\n\nScore the final valid representation through compatibility hooks. Define rank as one plus the number of candidates preceding the target under score descending/item ID ascending. Define margin as target score minus mean fixed-negative score; rank and margin deltas are intervention minus baseline. Define top-10 overlap as intersection size divided by min(10,candidate_count). Report paired per-user results, per-category means, equal-weight macro means, history-length slices of 8–15, 16–31, and at least 32 items, training-frequency tertiles, and slice counts. Use 1,000 paired bootstrap replicates, resampling users within each category and averaging category differences equally; report percentile 95% intervals. These intervals condition on fitted models and fixed candidates and do not cover training variability, candidate variability, or adaptive selection.\n\nSave every module's training flag, use eval() with torch.inference_mode(), and restore flags in finally. Use dedicated NumPy and PyTorch generators; protect any unavoidable global PyTorch RNG consumption with torch.random.fork_rng, including the active CUDA device. Do not consume a stateful training loader without preserving its sampler state; use a separate deterministic diagnostic loader for the existing margin probe. Gradient probes, if later added, need a separate gradient-enabled context.\n\nFor the required twenty training users, retain any externally specified user identities; otherwise choose five/category deterministically and log them. Encode each history once, score the entire category catalog in chunks, exclude padding and all seen items, and merge exact running top-k results. Use score descending/item ID ascending consistently at chunk pruning and merging; return fewer than k items if necessary. Also compare sampled and catalog target ranks on the diagnostic cases, labeling these settings separately and treating model-order agreement as unavailable until multiple fitted models exist.\n\nLog configurations, user IDs, prefix cutoffs, perturbation indices, candidate IDs, matching discrepancies, measured runtime, and all trial outcomes beneath the trial directory. Keep DATA_DIR=os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, four dataset routes, single-GPU execution, and SASREC_EPOCHS default 200. Charge all diagnostics and training to the shared 43,200 GPU-second budget. Predeclare one subsequent ablation, such as a scalar-gated recent-history residual, before inspecting its validation result. Architecture changes must update the predictor writer. Selection remains the host's unchanged 200-user/category, 99-negative, evaluation-seed-42 macro validation HR@10. Do not claim generalization from training-prefix perturbations or correct adaptive validation bias using ordinary bootstrap intervals.",
  "pseudocode": "run_full_diagnosis(train_loader):\n    save all module training flags\n    initialize independent diagnostic RNGs and fixed configuration\n    try:\n        view.model.eval()\n        with protected RNG state and inference_mode():\n            measure existing embedding and margin probes using isolated loader state\n            build deterministic eligible training-prefix cases in each category\n            cache candidates and original seen-item exclusions\n            for case:\n                construct frequency-matched recent, older, and random removals\n                construct separate last-item removal\n                construct local shuffle preserving final item and occupied positions\n                score baseline and repacked interventions\n                if explicit position/mask hook is supported:\n                    score position-preserving removals\n                else:\n                    record unavailable-control reason\n                record ranks, margins, overlaps, slices, and matching discrepancies\n                score baseline and interventions against fixed eligible catalog in chunks\n            aggregate paired observations per user and category\n            compute equal-weight macro summaries and paired user-bootstrap intervals\n            generate exact unseen-catalog recommendations for five users/category\n            return {metrics, metric_definitions}\n    finally:\n        restore each module's saved training flag\n\ndiagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis):\n    include measured metrics, definitions, counts, configuration, and missing controls\n    request evidence-linked findings with in-sample and perturbation caveats\n    distinguish score sensitivity, ranking quality, and unobserved preference intent\n    forbid unsupported compatibility, ownership, dislike, or simulator-trait claims\n    return prompt",
