  "description": "Test a conservative final-position SASRec consistency penalty after deleting one older, semantically isolated history event, using matched accuracy and positional controls to distinguish harmful dependence from useful historical evidence.",
  "motivation": "Qualitative feedback suggests that isolated historical interactions may receive excessive influence, but neither semantic isolation nor recommendation turnover establishes harm. Importance-guided augmentation is established by EC4SRec, while CL4SRec, CoSeRec and DuoRec demonstrate that view construction and loss design matter. The research contribution is therefore the controlled diagnostic-to-intervention connection, rather than targeted deletion itself.",
  "implementation_notes": "Preserve DATA_DIR = os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], all four dataset routes, preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, original splits, and the environment-controlled 200-epoch default. Keep execution single-GPU and artifacts beneath the trial directory. Use no unavailable review, rating, commercial-attribute, category, ownership, or trait information.\n\nFit deterministic sparse TF–IDF vectors on titles/descriptions of training-history items, with lowercase word unigrams/bigrams, sublinear term frequency and L2 normalization. Record preprocessing parameters. Missing or empty text makes an event ineligible for semantic targeting; do not interpret a zero vector as isolation. For each training prefix, eligible events precede the final interaction and occupy its older half by chronological index. Require at least two eligible events with usable text. Define isolation as one minus maximum cosine similarity to another text-observed prefix event. Delete the highest-isolation eligible event, breaking ties by earliest position; reversed targeting deletes the lowest-isolation event. Treat these scores solely as observable proxies.\n\nConstruct a cached, seed-42 diagnostic cohort from earlier training prefixes, capped at 200 users/category. Hold out the final training interaction as the diagnostic target and use the preceding prefix without altering benchmark splits. These targets are diagnostic probes, not an independent estimate of model generalization, because the ordinary training procedure may have seen them. Generate fixed training-only candidate sets and reuse them across intervention arms. Select one random eligible deletion from the same relative-age quartile as the targeted event, repeating five times. Use the same histories and deletion count in every arm.\n\nEvaluate compacted deletion and position-preserving masking separately. For compaction, right-align survivors in the original padded window, retaining the final event and prediction index. For masking, retain original survivor position IDs, zero removed input representations, and explicitly block removed attention keys/values at every layer; setting an item ID to zero alone is insufficient. Preserve the model's causal mask and handle all-masked attention rows safely. Their difference measures sensitivity to repacking, including content-position interactions. Neither intervention establishes real-world causality.\n\nMeasure original and edited target rank, binary HR@10, top-10 overlap and displacement on identical candidates. Retain optional unrestricted and final-item-fixed shuffle diagnostics to distinguish earlier ordering from last-item dependence. Keep full-catalog checks bounded and separate from pinned selection; the native twenty training-user recommendations must remain unseen-item full-catalog recommendations. Support for intervention requires improved target ranking after targeted removal beyond matched random controls, with directionally consistent positional-control results. A dependence signal alone is insufficient. Report results by category and history length; if evidence is inconclusive, prioritize the baseline and do not claim that targeting is validated.\n\nUse ordinary SASRec supervision at all valid positions. For consistency, select the last valid supervised training position and construct its exact preceding context so that the target never enters either view. Remove one eligible event and preserve its final context event. Use the unique shared candidate support comprising the target and available sampled training negatives, excluding padding and contradictory target-negative duplicates. This training support is distinct from benchmark candidates. Optimize L_rec + lambda * T^2 * KL(stopgrad(softmax(z_clean/T)) || softmax(z_edited/T)); unseen alternatives are unlabeled, not verified dislikes. Compute both consistency branches with dropout disabled while retaining gradients for the edited branch, preventing dropout noise from confounding deletion. Restore training mode before subsequent supervised passes. Temperature and support size affect KL magnitude, so log both.\n\nPredeclare T=1, one deletion per eligible prefix, and lambda in {0, 0.01, 0.1}. Compare the baseline, targeted consistency, matched-random consistency and dropout-only consistency; compare targeted augmentation without consistency to distinguish augmentation supervision from invariance. Use the same lambda grid where applicable. Reversed targeting is initially a diagnostic control. Symmetric/non-detached KL and InfoNCE under identical targeting are follow-up controls, not prerequisites for a first feasible experiment; without them, make no claim that detached KL is superior. Record eligible-prefix rate and effective deletion fraction by history length. A two-deletion sensitivity study is deferred unless budget remains and the initial hypothesis is supported.\n\nUse matching hardware, seeds, epoch limits and checkpoint-evaluation schedules; allocate equal GPU-second ceilings to compared intervention families rather than assuming equal trial counts imply equal cost. Count training, diagnostics, debugging and failures against 43,200 GPU-seconds, and log every attempted configuration and best-so-far macro validation HR@10 versus consumed compute. Stop launching trials when their declared ceiling would exceed the remaining budget. Repeated validation reuse remains a source of selection bias despite these safeguards.\n\nSelect only using the pinned four-category macro validation HR@10, including the unchanged baseline. Save per-user binary outcomes on identical pinned candidates when available. Bootstrap paired users within each category for 2,000 seed-42 replicates and recompute the macro difference each time; report the 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles. These intervals describe user-sampling uncertainty conditional on trained models and fixed candidates, not seed variation or adaptive-search bias. One changed hit moves a category score by 0.005 and macro HR by 0.00125. Never access hidden test labels or change the host evaluator.\n\nPreserve DiagnosisProbe(view, device).run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), returning metrics and metric_definitions, and diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis), embedding both. Use view.model as the actual nn.Module, retain the SASRec compatibility hooks, and restore its prior mode in a finally block. Report valid counts and unavailable values for empty cohorts; retain existing margin diagnostics with explicit definitions and unavailable category lists. Describe the existing mean-cosine score as directional alignment, not proof of collapse. No inference architecture change is required; verify that the predictor writer exports the selected checkpoint and all four routes into working/predict.py.",
  "pseudocode": "Predeclare text settings, diagnostic cohort, control arms, lambda grid, checkpoints and GPU ceilings.\nFit training-only TF–IDF vectors; cache text availability and eligible prefix events.\nFor each cached earlier-training prefix:\n    construct targeted, age-matched random and reversed-target deletions\n    retain final observed item, target, readout index and candidate support\n    score clean, compacted and explicitly masked views\n    record paired target ranks/hits, overlap and displacement\nAssess whether removal improves target ranking beyond matched controls in both positional arms.\nIf evidence is inconclusive, retain baseline and label the targeting hypothesis unresolved.\nIf supported, run the predeclared feasible intervention comparisons:\n    initialize each model with training seed 42\n    for each batch:\n        L_rec = ordinary SASRec loss at every valid supervised position\n        identify the final valid supervised position and its preceding context\n        for eligible contexts, construct the assigned edited view\n        C = shared unique target-plus-training-negative support\n        disable dropout for both consistency branches\n        p = softmax(clean_scores[C] / T).detach()\n        q = softmax(edited_scores[C] / T)\n        restore training mode\n        L = L_rec + lambda * T^2 * mean(KL(p || q))\n        optimize L; account for GPU time\n    evaluate only at predeclared checkpoints with the pinned protocol\nSelect baseline or intervention by equal-weight macro validation HR@10.\nReport all category scores, paired differences, uncertainty, attempted configurations and consumed compute.\nExport the selected checkpoint through the existing predictor writer and verify all dataset routes.",
  "originality": {
    "score": 3,
    "positive": "The controlled link between measured harmful dependence and a narrowly targeted intervention is a useful experimental contribution.",
    "negative": "Importance-guided editing, sequence augmentation and consistency objectives already exist; detached output KL and semantic targeting do not by themselves establish novelty."
  },
  "future_potential": {
    "score": 6,
    "positive": "Can establish whether behavioral diagnostics help select useful interventions across heterogeneous recommendation domains.",
    "negative": "Semantic isolation can identify valuable emerging interests, and training-prefix diagnostics may fail to predict validation gains."
  },
  "code_difficulty": {
    "score": 6,
    "positive": "Reuses SASRec and its inference architecture, with sparse text features and a bounded edited-prefix training branch.",
    "negative": "Correct attention masking, position controls, target alignment, controlled dropout, paired diagnostics and compute accounting require substantially more work than the initial rating suggested."
  }
}


