  "implementation_notes": "Keep DATA_DIR=os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], all four dataset routes, official splits, preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, and the SASREC_EPOCHS environment default of 200. Store all outputs beneath the trial directory and charge training, diagnostics, debugging, and failed trials to the shared 43,200 GPU-second budget. Retain DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), and diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis). Access the actual trained module through view.model and use the compatibility encoding/scoring hooks. Preserve existing embedding and margin measurements and their limitations; report unavailable category metadata explicitly rather than producing category-based claims. Do not use unavailable ratings, reviews, prices, stores, item categories, or simulator traits.\n\nBefore observing intervention validation results, serialize a manifest containing diagnostic user IDs and prefix cutoffs, candidate lists, perturbation settings and seeds, intervention configurations, probe-direction predictions, checkpoint schedule, query cap, and diagnostic GPU-time cap. Construct diagnostic examples from public training histories only: one example per selected user, using the final training interaction as target and its preceding interactions as prefix. These are in-sample targets because baseline training may already have used them. Choose up to 32 eligible users per category deterministically; require at least three prefix items and at least 99 eligible negative items. Set k=min(3,max(1,floor(0.2*prefix_length)),prefix_length-1), and use five isolated shuffle/random-deletion seeds. Use stable cryptographic hashes of category, user ID, and replicate to derive RNG seeds; do not use Python's process-randomized hash or mutate global training/evaluation RNGs. Save actual selections to remove dependence on iteration order.\n\nSample 99 unique diagnostic negatives excluding padding, the target, and observed prefix items. Keep each user's candidate IDs and ordering identical across variants and checkpoints. These diagnostic exclusions are independent of the host evaluator: preserve its pinned users, 99 unique negatives, exclusions, seed 42, and ranking semantics exactly. Record exclusions and eligibility counts. For diagnostic ties, sort candidates by ascending item ID, then use a stable descending score sort; target rank is one plus the number of strictly higher scores plus equal-scoring candidates with smaller item ID. Use this same ordering for diagnostic top-10 sets. Do not assume the host uses this tie rule.\n\nRight-align compacted histories with zero left padding, preserving the baseline length L, integer dtype, and device. For ordinary SASRec use log2feats(sequence)[:, -1, :] as the final representation. Explicitly support shared candidate IDs [C] and per-user candidates [B,C], returning [B,C] scores without dropping the batch dimension when B=1. Prefix shuffles preserve the last item, occupied slots, prefix length, and item multiset. Recent/oldest/random compacted deletions remove k items from their respective pools; label recent deletion that removes the last item separately because it changes the immediate conditioning item.\n\nFor a position-controlled comparison, define a separate eligible pool excluding the last item and use the same k and deletion indices in compacted and position-preserving variants. Position-preserving removal retains survivor position IDs and masks deleted slots as attention keys/values at every layer while retaining the final item and readout position. Prevent all-masked attention rows and nonfinite outputs. Simply inserting zero IDs is insufficient unless the actual architecture masks those keys/values throughout every layer. If the compatibility hooks cannot express the required mask, report the probe as unsupported. This control reduces positional confounding but does not remove attention-renormalization or distribution-shift effects. Restore model mode in a finally block and verify unchanged-history controls reproduce scores within a recorded numerical tolerance.\n\nReport paired target-rank changes, target hit changes within the diagnostic candidates, top-10 set overlap, feature cosine similarity where defined, eligible user counts, and variation across perturbation seeds. Average replicates within users, then users within category, then the four categories equally. Define positive rank change as worse ranking and overlap as intersection size divided by 10. Do not silently omit a missing category from a four-category macro statistic. Use 1,000 paired user-bootstrap replicates within each category with an isolated seed and report percentile intervals; flag small samples and explain that these intervals exclude training-seed variability and adaptive-selection bias.\n\nFreeze a small intervention list before querying validation: unchanged SASRec, a fixed five-item input window, and uniform removal of 10% of nonfinal input tokens during training while preserving targets and positions. Only run interventions after explicitly budgeting their estimated training cost; retain the same seeds and training/checkpoint schedule. Compare diagnostic predictions against intervention-minus-baseline pinned per-category HR@10 changes, alongside training-loss and training-derived recency/popularity summaries. With few interventions and four categories, use descriptive prediction errors and sign agreement rather than fitted significance claims. Separately trained last-item, uniform-pooling, and fixed-recency additive item-to-candidate controls are useful explanatory extensions only if budget remains; freeze their settings before validation. Stop expanding probes if they show no incremental predictive value. Sensitivity is not an objective to maximize, and neither fixed candidates nor bootstrap intervals prevents adaptive validation overfitting. Make no claims of improved HR@10 until measured. The diagnostic-only phase requires no predictor changes; any subsequent architecture or inference change must also update the predictor writer so working/predict.py loads and reproduces the trained model.",
