INFO:self_evolverec:The new idea for model in iteration 2:
{
  "description": "Add a recent-conditioned scalar gate between shared-weight SASRec encodings of the full observed prefix and its last ten nonpadding items. Normalize branch representations before their convex mixture and use the existing item dot-product scorer. Treat this as an incremental context-selection hypothesis whose value must exceed cheaper truncation, pooling, and fixed-mixture controls.",
  "motivation": "The selected idea explicitly targets two user-facing failures: older or isolated interactions being interpreted as current demand, and broadly related historical interests competing with more specific recent matches. Preserving a full-history branch keeps persistent interests available. The model-facing target is weak positive–negative separation, particularly Electronics’ negative tenth-percentile margin and 14.95% wrong-ranking rate. These diagnostics do not establish that historical distraction causes errors. Recency cannot reliably identify compatibility, replacement purpose, or satisfied durable-device needs, and a recent isolated interaction can itself be misleading. The objective-only gBCE alternative remains useful, but it does not explicitly address both required user-facing failures.",
  "implementation_notes": "Preserve four category-specific models, dataset routing, original splits, preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, and DATA_DIR = os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR']. Keep baseline optimizer, loss, sampling, and the SASREC_EPOCHS environment setting, whose host default is 200, for the first architectural comparison. Train on one GPU and save all artifacts beneath the trial directory. Never access hidden test data or fetch pretrained weights or additional datasets. Only public histories, titles, descriptions, and timestamps are available; generic schema examples do not authorize using ratings, reviews, categories, prices, or stores.\n\nDefine the initial recent window W=10. For each valid supervised position t, use sequence items only through t. Retain the full branch’s baseline causal encoding and its existing positional convention. Construct the recent branch independently: gather the last W observed nonpadding items ending at t, left-pad to W slots, assign position IDs 0 through W−1, run every shared encoder layer, and retain the final-slot feature. Deployment uses exactly this W-slot construction. Parameter sharing includes attention, feed-forward blocks, item embeddings, and the positional embedding table; the recent branch uses the table’s first W entries. This deliberately resets recent positions and must be distinguished from preserving full-branch positions in an ablation. No representation may be computed from the batch endpoint and reused for earlier targets. Dense local masking is not an equivalent shortcut: positional embeddings differ, and stacked layers can transmit information from outside a nominal window.\n\nThe previous instruction to reuse full features whenever prefix length is at most W is withdrawn: full and recent position IDs, padding lengths, and dropout masks can differ. Reuse is valid only when the complete inputs and positional conventions match, and stochastic-mask equivalence is also required for exact training equality. Evaluation parity checks run in eval mode. The gate’s length input is the count of observed nonpadding items in the supplied prefix, capped by the baseline sequence length; do not substitute a full-user-history count unavailable through the inference contract.\n\nFor hidden width d, normalize branch outputs as z=h/sqrt(mean(h²)+1e−6), using no additional branch-specific affine parameters. Set g=sigmoid(Linear(GELU(Linear(concat(z_full,z_recent,log1p(length)),d)),1)); initialize the final linear weights and bias to zero. Form fused=(1−g)*z_full+g*z_recent and retain the baseline candidate dot-product scorer. This normalization changes baseline score scale, so include a normalized full-only control and identical normalization in mixture comparisons. Record raw branch norms, g, weighted contribution norms, and branch cosine similarity; normalization cannot prevent cancellation or establish that gate variation reflects intent. The gate sees no target identity, future timestamp, simulator trait, or unavailable metadata.\n\nFor B sequences and T supervised endpoints, explicit recent windows with input length W require approximately O(BTD(W²d+Wd²)) dense arithmetic across D layers. Conventional saved recent activations grow approximately as O(BTD(Wd+hW²)) for h attention heads, in addition to the full branch. Parameter sharing does not remove this cost. Chunking forward calls alone does not bound retained autograd activations. Use nonreentrant checkpointing of scalar recent-window chunk losses, passing integer windows and full features into checkpointed functions and constructing recent embeddings inside. Preserve dropout RNG during recomputation. Sum chunk losses divided by the total number of valid supervised positions, then backpropagate and step once. Keep the baseline loss’s original positive/negative weighting inside each position loss. This retains the full-branch graph and checkpoint inputs while avoiding all saved recent activations; it does not eliminate their arithmetic. Validate gradients against an unchunked small reference with dropout disabled. Immediate chunk backward is an alternative only if full-branch graph retention or recomputation is handled explicitly and the same global loss normalization is used.\n\nUse a staged, prespecified search rather than an exhaustive ablation grid. Before runs, record a concrete allocation within the shared 43,200 GPU-second budget, checkpoint evaluation cadence, deterministic stopping rule, minimum practically meaningful macro improvement, and tie rule favoring lower compute. Charge training, diagnostics, debugging, and failures. The host default remains 200 epochs; any early-stopping variation must be declared. Establish unchanged SASRec, normalized full-only, and recent-only W=10 controls first. If budget permits, test recent-only W=5 and W=20 and exponential recency pooling with weights proportional to exp(−0.1*r), where r is backward position distance. Pooling uses observed item embeddings with the same normalization and scorer; label it a separate representation baseline. For identical dual branches, compare fixed weights g in {0.25,0.5,0.75}, a learned global sigmoid scalar initialized at 0.5, and the context gate. Apply identical normalization, windows, loss, and positional conventions. Compare model size and GPU seconds; a larger single encoder or two-encoder fixed ensemble is a secondary capacity control if affordable. Do not combine gBCE with fusion in the first comparison. Record every evaluated configuration and checkpoint.\n\nCheck shortcut learning using validation-only interventions without retraining or changing host evaluation: remove the length input, inspect gate behavior by observed prefix length and target training-popularity strata, and deterministically permute gate values across validation users within category. Permuting gates tests contextual assignment but creates an intervention distribution shift; interpret it as supporting evidence rather than proof. Train no gate on validation labels. Recent position resetting, branch scale, popularity, or increased computation could explain gains without improved intent modeling. Limit adaptive tuning and freeze the final selection before independent held-out evaluation.\n\nSelect only by the pinned macro validation HR@10 with 200 users/category, 99 unique negatives, and evaluation seed 42. Report every category score and GPU seconds. The previous inspiration’s 0.5363 is a reported historical score, not evidence for this architecture. If the unchanged evaluator exposes paired per-user hit indicators, compute macro difference as the average of four category mean paired differences. Use 10,000 stratified paired bootstrap replicates with seed 42, resampling users within category while preserving paired outcomes; report the difference in percentage points and its 95% percentile interval. These exploratory intervals condition on fitted models, fixed categories, and fixed candidate panels; they exclude training-seed and candidate-sampling variability and do not correct adaptive winner selection. If paired indicators are unavailable, report that limitation without modifying the host evaluator. Optional prespecified binary comparisons may report A-only/B-only hit counts and exact McNemar tests; apply Holm correction if making four category-specific inferential claims.\n\nKeep view.model as the actual trained nn.Module and retain item_emb, log2feats(sequence), predict_score(features, items), user_train, meta_data, and review_data. Make log2feats return fused B×L features, with padding outputs masked, so predict_score remains a stateless item dot-product function. Implement one shared window-construction and fusion routine for training, diagnostics, and export. Add an internal final-feature path that computes only the last endpoint’s recent window for validation and inference; verify its equality to the corresponding log2feats output. This avoids recomputing every training endpoint during deployment.\n\nRetain DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), and diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis). The loader remains (None, sequence, positive, negative), with B×L tensors. Measure complete-score mean/p10 margins, wrong-ranking and small-margin rates with eps=0.5, embedding cosine similarity, gate values, raw branch norms, effective contribution norms, and margins stratified by observed prefix length. Use deterministic diagnostic sampling and report the measured position count. Return metrics and metric_definitions. Mark category breakdowns unavailable rather than inventing item categories. The interpretation prompt must include measured values, definitions, and missing-data limitations. Similarity values near 0.5 do not establish severe collapse, and training margins do not establish validation improvement.\n\nUpdate the predictor writer to export the shared encoder, gate, normalization, configuration, item mappings, positional convention, and identical recent-window construction into working/predict.py. Preserve category routing and evaluator masking rules. For simulator recommendations to twenty real training users, five/category, score the full catalog in chunks and mask observed items according to the established contract. Verify causal prefix invariance by modifying suffix items without moving earlier position IDs, checkpoint reload parity, generated-predictor parity, final-feature-path parity, and candidate/full-catalog score agreement. Qualitative assessment must distinguish uncertain relevance from explicit mismatch; claims about immediate-choice acceptance or compatibility require actual observations.",
  "pseudocode": "Set preprocessing seed=0, training seed=42; preserve four-category routing.\nPredeclare budget, checkpoint cadence, stopping and tie rules, and comparisons.\n\nrecent_windows(sequence, endpoints, W=10):\n    for each endpoint (b,t):\n        prefix = nonpadding(sequence[b,:t+1])\n        yield left_pad(prefix[-W:], W), position_ids=0..W-1\n\nnormalize(h):\n    return h / sqrt(mean(h*h, dim=-1, keepdim=True) + 1e-6)\n\nfor category in FOUR_CATEGORIES:\n    preserve baseline splits, item mappings, and environment epoch setting\n    initialize shared SASRec encoder and gate; zero gate's final layer\n    for baseline training batch:\n        valid = positions with valid positive targets\n        full = shared_encoder(sequence, baseline_position_ids)\n        total_loss = 0\n        for chunk in partition(valid):\n            integer_windows = recent_windows(sequence, chunk, W=10)\n            chunk_loss = checkpoint_nonreentrant(\n                function(integer_windows, full_features_at_chunk):\n                    recent = shared_encoder(integer_windows, positions=0..W-1)[:,-1]\n                    z_full = normalize(full_features_at_chunk)\n                    z_recent = normalize(recent)\n                    g = sigmoid(gate(z_full, z_recent, log1p(observed_prefix_length)))\n                    fused = (1-g)*z_full + g*z_recent\n                    scores = dot(fused, matching_positive_negative_embeddings)\n                    return sum(baseline_per_position_loss(scores)),\n                integer_windows, full[chunk], preserve_rng_state=True)\n            total_loss += chunk_loss / number_of_valid_positions\n        backward(total_loss)\n        optimizer.step(); optimizer.zero_grad()\n    evaluate only through unchanged pinned validation protocol\n    save architecture, weights, mappings, configuration, and matching predictor\n\nlog2feats(sequence):\n    compute full branch and independent recent window at every valid endpoint\n    normalize, gate, fuse, mask padding; return B×L features\n\nfinal_features(sequence):\n    compute full final feature and only the final recent window\n    apply identical normalization, gate and fusion\n\nDiagnosisProbe.run_full_diagnosis(train_loader):\n    measure complete scorer margins, embedding similarity, gate and branch statistics\n    return metrics and metric_definitions; mark unavailable breakdowns explicitly\n\ndiagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis):\n    return prompt containing measured metrics, definitions and limitations\n\nVerify with dropout disabled:\n    chunked-checkpoint gradients match an unchunked reference\n    suffix changes cannot affect earlier features at unchanged position IDs\n    final_features equals log2feats at the last valid position\n    saved/reloaded model and generated predictor reproduce scores\n    candidate scores equal corresponding full-catalog entries\n\nRun declared controls before interpreting contextual gating.\nSelect only by pinned four-category macro validation HR@10.\nIf paired hits are available, report exploratory stratified paired bootstrap intervals.\nFreeze final choice; leave held-out evaluation to the host.",
  "originality": {
    "score": 2,
    "positive": "Provides a controlled adaptation with explicit causal windows, shared parameters, and deployment parity under the exact benchmark contract.",
    "negative": "Adaptive long/short fusion, including comparisons with fixed mixtures, is established in SLi-Rec and subsequent work; the gate itself is not a new scientific mechanism."
  },
  "future_potential": {
