INFO:self_evolverec:Research reports (2 report(s)):
INFO:self_evolverec:  Report 1: SASRec with a small recent-context gate and multi-negative training is the strongest first experiment. This is a hypothesis; no benchmark improvement has been measured.

**Insights from the starting point.** (1) SASRec already models ordered context, making a small extension feasible. Retaining its encoder limits implementation risk. (2) Electronics has 14.95% sampled wrong rankings and a −0.4812 tenth-percentile margin. This motivates stronger candidate discrimination, but does not establish HR@10 gains. (3) Users report both broad, unsupported recommendations and stale interests. Recent, repeated behavioral evidence could help both, although histories cannot establish ownership or compatibility.

**Insights from related work.** (1) Dross Into Gold and gSASRec motivate testing richer negative sets before replacing the encoder. Their results do not establish an optimum here. (2) MISER and RecGRELA motivate separating recent and persistent preferences. A small gate offers a cheaper initial experiment than multiple interest encoders. (3) UFNRec cautions that difficult negatives can be unlabeled positives. Begin with uniform sampling and avoid interpreting uncertain recommendations as dislikes.

**Framework and directions.**

| Direction | Existing mechanism | Proposed test | Evidence gap |
|---|---|---|---|
| Candidate discrimination | SASRec sampled loss; gSASRec | Uniform multi-negative softmax | Four-category HR@10 |
| Current-interest evidence | MISER; RecGRELA | Gated recent residual | Immediate-choice acceptance |
| Specific content evidence | Sceptre | Scratch-trained title residual | Compatibility remains unknown |

**Candidate ideas.** Scores are originality / potential / user alignment / model alignment / implementation difficulty; difficulty increases with complexity. Each assessment gives a positive (+) and limitation (−).

1. **Uniform multi-negative softmax:** 3/7/4/9/3. Originality: + grounded adaptation, − established; potential: + reusable, − benchmark-dependent; user: + finer ranking, − no explicit recency; model: + targets confusion, − false negatives; difficulty: + small loss change, − candidate masking.
2. **Recent-evidence gate with multi-negative softmax:** **5/8/8/8/5**. Originality: + focused combination, − familiar components; potential: + adaptive context, − sparse-history uncertainty; user: + addresses stale and broad matches, − cannot verify practical fit; model: + trains competing candidates, − margins may not generalize; difficulty: + small module, − predictor parity required.
3. **Scratch-trained title residual:** 4/6/7/6/6. Originality: + available-text adaptation, − established fusion; potential: + specific identity cues, − sparse vocabulary; user: + finer matches, − shared words can mislead; model: + additional discrimination, − shortcut risk; difficulty: + lightweight encoder, − tokenization artifacts.

**Selected algorithm.** At 0% progress, choose idea 2. Fuse SASRec features with an attention-pooled recent prefix through a learned gate, then train against 64 uniform negatives. This explicitly targets stale/isolated-interest reliance and broad matches lacking recent support; multi-negative competition targets weak lower-tail separation. These are intended mechanisms, not verified outcomes.

Use causal prefixes, exclude known training positives from negative pools, and preserve all category routing. Compare baseline, loss-only, gate-only, and combined variants within budget. Select exclusively by pinned macro validation HR@10. Keep diagnostic definitions explicit and regenerate the predictor with identical architecture.
INFO:self_evolverec:  Report 2: The first experiment remains SASRec with a recent-context residual and uniform multi-negative training. Benefits are hypotheses; no benchmark improvement has been measured.

**Insights from the starting point.** (1) SASRec already models causal context. Preserve its actual RecHarness implementation to isolate changes. (2) Electronics has 14.95% sampled wrong rankings and a −0.4812 tenth-percentile margin. This motivates candidate discrimination, without establishing HR@10 gains. (3) Users identify stale interests and broad, unsupported matches. Recent evidence addresses both provisionally, but cannot establish ownership or compatibility.

**Insights from related work.** (1) Dross Into Gold and gSASRec support richer negative sets; candidate softmax and gBCE remain distinct alternatives. Neither establishes an optimum here. (2) MISER, RecGRELA and especially TLSRec motivate recent/persistent fusion. Their overlap lowers the proposal’s originality. (3) UFNRec highlights false-negative risk. Unobserved items and uncertain recommendations are not demonstrated dislikes.

**Framework and directions.**

| Direction | Existing mechanism | Proposed test | Evidence gap |
|---|---|---|---|
| Candidate discrimination | SASRec; gSASRec | Uniform multi-negative softmax | Pinned HR@10 |
| Current-interest evidence | MISER; RecGRELA; TLSRec | Zero-initialized recent residual | Immediate-choice acceptance |
| Specific content evidence | Sceptre | Scratch-trained title residual | Compatibility unknown |

**Candidate ideas.** Scores are originality / potential / user alignment / model alignment / implementation difficulty; higher difficulty means more work. Each assessment includes a positive (+) and limitation (−).

1. **Uniform multi-negative softmax:** 3/7/4/9/4. Originality: + grounded adaptation, − established; potential: + reusable, − benchmark-dependent; user: + discrimination, − no recency; model: + targets confusion, − false negatives; difficulty: + small loss change, − exact sampling and gradients.
2. **Recent residual with multi-negative softmax:** **4/7/8/8/6**. Originality: + focused combination, − close fusion precedents; potential: + adaptive context, − sparse-history uncertainty; user: + targets stale and broad matches, − cannot verify fit; model: + candidate competition, − margins may not generalize; difficulty: + small module, − sampling, gradients and predictor parity.
3. **Scratch-trained title residual:** 4/6/7/6/6. Originality: + available-text adaptation, − established fusion; potential: + identity cues, − sparse vocabulary; user: + specificity, − terminology shortcuts; model: + additional evidence, − memorization; difficulty: + lightweight encoder, − tokenization artifacts.

**Selected algorithm.** At 0% progress, retain idea 2, with loss-only as the first control. Pool five nonpadding causal tokens and add a gated, branch-normalized residual scaled by a parameter initialized exactly zero. This preserves baseline outputs initially. The branch targets stale/isolated-interest reliance and broad matches lacking recent support; 64-negative candidate softmax targets weak lower-tail separation. None demonstrates compatibility or immediate-choice improvement.

Compare unchanged baseline, loss-only, branch-only and combined variants; reserve normalization/capacity and pooling controls for subsequent budgeted work. Carry explicit user identities for history exclusions while preserving the diagnostic tuple through an adapter. Normalize chunked losses by total valid positions and propagate both tied-embedding gradient paths. Freeze validation cadence, budget and tie-breaking beforehand. Select only by four-category macro HR@10 against 99 fixed negatives, report category deltas, and label adaptive comparisons exploratory. Preserve diagnostic definitions, seeds, routing and predictor-writer parity.
