  "implementation_notes": "Preserve the exact base score and existing gate; alpha multiplies the complete deployed residual, avoiding double gating. Verify the architecture prerequisite from the actual implementation and checkpoint during host-side implementation. Do not infer a residual from aggregate complete-minus-raw margins. Require an explicit architecture-specific scoring hook receiving history or explicitly retained branch context: predict_score(features, items) alone does not guarantee the necessary information. Do not use mutable last-history caches, which can corrupt batched or interleaved inference. Preserve the compatibility interface through a documented adapter to the same complete deployed scoring path.\n\nFreeze alpha_grid=[0.0,0.5,1.0], one checkpoint, and the tie rule before obtaining validation results. Use the baseline checkpoint selection policy unchanged; do not select a different epoch for each alpha. If training is required, retain preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, and the environment-controlled SASREC_EPOCHS default of 200. Compare identical histories, exclusion masks, validation users, candidate IDs, and evaluator tie handling. Select one global alpha by equal-weight macro validation HR@10; break exact ties toward the largest alpha. Do not fit category-specific scales in this experiment. A positive global temperature or candidate-independent offset preserves rankings and cannot itself improve HR@10. Use raw logits without adding a sigmoid to prediction.\n\nBefore validation, require alpha=1 score reconstruction and checkpoint-reload/model/export parity on deterministic public training inputs. Check candidate-permutation equivariance, batch-versus-single consistency, finite outputs, correct category/item routing, and chunked-versus-unchunked scoring. Use float32 comparisons with rtol=1e-5 and atol=1e-6; require matching top-10 outputs under the same tie rule and investigate discrepancies before proceeding. Include short, padded, and maximum-length histories. For causal SASRec, changing future tokens must not alter earlier features; history-order invariance is not a required property. These are adaptations of recommendation testing principles, not asserted built-in RecPack Tests checks.\n\nRecord every alpha's per-category and macro validation HR@10. Where the host exposes paired outcomes or scores, also report top-10 turnover, target-rank changes, boundary gaps, and paired user-hit changes. Otherwise mark those validation diagnostics unavailable; compute explicitly labeled training-side sensitivity separately without changing the evaluator. One changed hit among 200 users in one category changes macro HR@10 by 0.00125. Do not fabricate uncertainty intervals when paired outcomes are absent. Any intervals condition on the fixed candidates and training seed and do not account for adaptive selection. Selecting alpha on validation can overfit; no selected validation gain establishes held-out improvement.\n\nRetain DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), and diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis). Use view.model as the actual trained module; preserve view.item_emb, log2feats, predict_score, user_train, meta_data, and review_data. Process at most four batches and the first 512 valid supervised positions per batch, excluding padding. Use evaluation mode and no_grad; restore every submodule's original training flag in finally, including on exceptions. Preserve existing pairwise and geometry measurements, add branch-supported ranking sensitivity, and return metrics and metric_definitions with observation counts, units, signs, and null plus reasons for unavailable measurements. Explain that raw margins depend on score scale and mean embedding cosine alone does not establish collapse. Keep unavailable item-category breakdowns empty. The interpreter prompt must embed measured metrics and definitions, distinguish training sensitivity from validation benefit, and prohibit invented metadata or latent traits.\n\nKeep graph construction training-only. For an existing user-excluding or cross-fitted branch, preserve its established training and deployment policies during the alpha sweep. Excluding each user's entire history prevents that user's interactions from contributing to their training graph features, but deployment graph support, normalization, and residual scale can still differ. Record those policies and retain the observed shift diagnostic. Changing graph construction or aligning training/deployment graphs is a separate experiment. Fixed-checkpoint alpha=0 does not substitute for training a branch-free baseline: backbone and residual may co-adapt. Architectural claims require independently trained matched models with equal tuning opportunities and accounted costs.\n\nSave alpha, score decomposition version, architecture configuration, checkpoint identity, graph artifacts and construction policy, item mappings, and category routing beneath the trial directory. Update the predictor writer so working/predict.py reconstructs the trained architecture and selected scoring path; make diagnostic default scores use the same alpha. Separately produce the prescribed full-catalog unseen-item lists for twenty real training users, five per category, using training histories and chunked scoring. Without relevance labels, these lists support descriptive concentration, coverage, and stability only. Train-only popularity and history-length strata may reveal shortcuts but cannot establish current intent or causal preference.\n\nRetain Movies_and_TV, Industrial_and_Scientific, Electronics, and CDs_and_Vinyl; DATA_DIR = os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR']; unchanged splits; and the pinned host evaluator with 200 validation users/category, 99 unique negatives, and evaluation seed 42. Use single-GPU execution and record training, diagnostics, debugging, failures, and all comparison costs against the shared 43,200 GPU-second budget. Freeze a remaining-budget cap before execution; do not launch optional retraining without enough budget. Use only public histories, titles, descriptions, and timestamps, with no external data or pretrained weights. Ignore unavailable ratings, review text, prices, stores, item categories, and simulator traits. Read no hidden tests. If the branch prerequisite fails, stop this calibration experiment, preserve the valid SASRec predictor, and report the missing prerequisite; history dropout remains a separately registered candidate rather than an unreported fallback.",
  "pseudocode": "freeze alpha_grid = [0.0, 0.5, 1.0], tie_rule = largest_alpha\nfreeze checkpoint policy, artifact manifest, and remaining GPU-time cap\nobtain prescribed baseline checkpoint without changing splits or seeds\nif actual model/checkpoint/predictor lack an existing transition branch:\n    record calibration_unavailable with reason\n    preserve valid baseline predictor\n    stop this experiment\nrequire explicit score_components(history, candidates)\nfreeze checkpoint and existing graph construction policies\nbase, gated_residual = score_components(public_training_inputs)\nassert base + gated_residual reconstructs original raw scores\nfor alpha in alpha_grid:\n    configure score = base + alpha * gated_residual\n    write trial-local predictor and complete restoration metadata\n    verify reload/export scores, rankings, routing, batching, and candidate permutation\n    obtain pinned host validation HR@10 for all four categories\n    record category HR@10, equal-weight macro HR@10, availability of paired diagnostics, and costs\nselected_alpha = argmax((macro_HR10[alpha], alpha))\nexport selected predictor and matching diagnostic configuration\nverify final export parity\nrecord all comparisons and descriptive twenty-user full-catalog lists\n\nrun_full_diagnosis(train_loader):\n    saved_flags = capture_each_submodule_training_flag(view.model)\n    try:\n        view.model.eval()\n        with no_grad():\n            measure existing pairwise and geometry probes\n            bound work to four batches and 512 valid positions per batch\n            measure branch sensitivity only when scoring context is available\n            attach counts, definitions, units, signs, missing reasons, and limitations\n        return {'metrics': metrics, 'metric_definitions': definitions}\n    finally:\n        restore_each_submodule_training_flag(saved_flags)\n\ndiagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis):\n    embed measured metrics and definitions\n    request interpretation grounded in those observations\n    distinguish training fit, inference sensitivity, validation, and unknown preferences",
