{
  "description": "Add a rank-32, recency-weighted directed item-transition residual to each category's SASRec model. Jointly train combined scores, preserve the original routing and evaluation protocol, and treat this as a controlled extension of established factorization methods rather than a novel model family.",
  "motivation": "Qualitative feedback suggests that broad historical similarity sometimes outweighs recent specific evidence. An explicit history branch makes that hypothesis testable, but improved recency sensitivity need not improve ranking or establish immediate purchase intent. FMC, PCTM and FOSSIL provide distinct precedents; none establishes gains for this SASRec combination on the four requested datasets.",
  "implementation_notes": "Preserve preprocessing seed=0, training seed=42, all four datasets, original splits and dataset routing. DATA_DIR remains os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR']; all checkpoints, configurations and generated working/predict.py remain beneath the trial directory. Respect SASREC_EPOCHS, whose supplied environment default is 200, and retain single-GPU execution. No hidden test access, extra datasets or pretrained weights.\n\nUse separate rank-32 source and target embeddings with padding_idx=0. Initialize source coordinates independently with standard deviation 1/sqrt(32), target embeddings to zero, and the per-category learned scalar gate so softplus(gate)=1. This makes the initial residual zero while permitting initial task gradients into target rows; source and gate task gradients initially vanish and can develop after targets update. Both factors must not start at zero. This is a reproducible initialization choice, not an established optimum. Log branch/base score RMS and update magnitudes before changing initialization.\n\nSet tau=5 and decay=exp(-1/tau), measured in retained interaction positions rather than elapsed time. For each supplied truncated sequence, initialize float32 numerator n and mass z to zero. At position t, m=(sequence[:,t]!=0); update n=where(m, decay*n+source(sequence[:,t]), n), z=where(m, decay*z+1, z), and h=n/z.clamp_min(1e-8). Broadcast masks over features. Stack outputs without detaching. Padding contributes no mass or decay; the current observed item contributes when predicting its next item. Reset state for every supplied sequence. Repeated items contribute repeatedly, which may amplify preference evidence or popularity and repeated-consumption shortcuts.\n\nKeep the baseline loss, optimizer, sampling, history length and contextual regularization initially. Define the baseline regularizer explicitly over its original parameters so adding tables does not silently introduce a full-table penalty. Apply no explicit L2 or weight decay to the new factors and gate in the initial trial; retain applicable original optimizer settings on baseline parameters. Inspect whether baseline regularization is squared, unsquared, coupled or decoupled, and record it. If later adding branch regularization, declare its expression, reduction and coefficient, and avoid accidentally applying both a loss penalty and optimizer shrinkage. Adam normalization can mitigate gradient scaling but does not guarantee useful updates under epsilon, noise or shrinkage.\n\nHave log2feats return concatenated contextual features of dimension D and history features of dimension 32, giving B x L x (D+32). predict_score splits at D and computes the contextual dot product plus softplus(gate) times the history/target dot product. Support paired items B x L x 2 and final features B x (D+32) with candidates B x K through the same scoring function. Preserve view.model as the actual trained module and view.item_emb as the contextual item table; document that its alignment diagnostic excludes the transition tables. Adapt diagnostic feature-dimension assumptions.\n\nRetain DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), and diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis). Return metrics and metric_definitions grounded in measured quantities, with valid-position counts and null values for unavailable observations. Restore the model's previous training mode after diagnostics. Use only permitted histories, timestamps, titles and descriptions; ignore the generic input examples containing unavailable ratings, reviews, prices, stores or item categories. Raw margins remain score-scale dependent. Popularity strata must derive exclusively from training interactions.\n\nIn eval mode without gradients, verify recurrence against a direct weighted sum; test left padding, empty and singleton histories, and truncation. Compare scores[:, -1, :] against final-feature scoring on identical candidates, and repeat after strict checkpoint reload using the serialized architecture, dimensions, decay and gate. For prefix comparisons, hold retained histories and positional indices identical. Empty histories must produce finite outputs; their contextual behavior must be explicitly defined if the baseline attention implementation cannot handle all-padding sequences. Update the predictor writer to reconstruct the full architecture and load all parameters and persistent buffers.\n\nPrioritize a matched baseline-versus-residual comparison. Predeclare the maximum number of fits and validation queries in the trial configuration before execution; no numeric trial cap was supplied in the report. If budget remains, compare last-item, uniform and exponential pooling with identical factors, loss and optimization, followed by a parameter-matched wider SASRec. Transition-only, tied-table and frozen-branch blend controls are useful later, not mandatory initial searches. Evaluate lambda=0 ablation and combined scoring on identical validation users and candidates; do not interpret inference ablation as equivalent to separately training SASRec. Record per-category and macro HR@10, training cost, gate/norm trajectories, residual/base RMS and rank changes. Account for all training and diagnostic GPU time within the shared 43,200 GPU-second budget. Keep the host evaluator unchanged. Limit adaptive validation reuse; paired within-category user bootstrap intervals, if available, are descriptive after selection and conditional on fixed candidates and seed. Twenty simulator users support qualitative inspection only.",
  "pseudocode": "For each routed category:\n  construct baseline SASRec with its original settings\n  construct source[items,32] ~ Normal(0,1/sqrt(32)); target[items,32] = 0\n  zero padding rows; set gate = inverse_softplus(1); decay = exp(-1/5)\n  retain baseline optimizer settings; explicitly exclude new factors/gate from decay\n  for each baseline batch (None, sequence, positive, negative):\n    context = causal_SASRec(sequence)\n    n = zeros(B,32,float32); z = zeros(B,1,float32)\n    history = []\n    for t in sequence positions:\n      m = (sequence[:,t] != 0)[:,None]\n      n = where(m, decay*n + source(sequence[:,t]).float(), n)\n      z = where(m, decay*z + 1, z)\n      history.append(n / clamp_min(z,1e-8))\n    features = concatenate(context, stack(history), last_dimension)\n    scores = shared_predict_score(features, stack(positive,negative))\n    optimize original loss at valid next-item positions plus original baseline regularizer\n  save full state and architecture configuration; generate matching predictor\n  verify direct recurrence, final-position scoring and strict reload equivalence\nCompare baseline and combined model under pinned validation; select by equal-weight macro HR@10.\nIf the declared budget permits, run pooling and capacity controls with identical evaluation candidates.\nInterpret branch ablation, perturbations and qualitative feedback as diagnostics, not user-intent measurements.",
