INFO:self_evolverec:The new idea for model in iteration 2:
{
  "description": "Add an independently encoded five-interaction pathway to per-dataset SASRec, mix its features with full-history features through a learned scalar gate, and train with 128 uniform negatives using the exact published gBCE objective. Evaluate the components separately before selecting the combination.",
  "motivation": "Recent-context gating addresses older or isolated interactions treated as current demand and broad historical associations displacing recent specific interests. Multiple-negative training addresses weak positive–negative separation, especially Electronics' 14.95% sampled wrong-ranking rate and negative lower-decile margin. These mechanisms cannot establish equipment compatibility, exhausted demand, ownership satisfaction, or user dislikes. Independent component ablations are essential because the supplied diagnostics do not establish which mechanism will improve macro validation HR@10.",
  "implementation_notes": "Preserve DATA_DIR = os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, all four category routes, existing splits and pinned evaluator. Write artifacts beneath the trial directory. Preserve baseline architecture dimensions, optimizer, batch size and other hyperparameters unless explicitly listed as a variant. Use int(os.environ.get('SASREC_EPOCHS', '200')) as the epoch ceiling, subject to the shared 43,200 GPU-second budget; run all training single-GPU.\n\nArchitecture: share item embeddings, transformer blocks, normalization, positional embedding table and candidate scoring weights. For each valid supervised position, independently gather the raw item-ID suffix containing at most five interactions, left-pad to five, and encode it without access to full-history hidden states. Both branches assign position IDs 0 through branch_length-1 to their padded input slots; recent windows always use slots 0 through 4 during training and inference. Full-history inputs preserve the baseline's fixed-length positional policy. Padding positions are masked. The recent branch uses the final window feature; the full branch uses the corresponding causal full-history feature. Set g = sigmoid(w^T concat(full, recent) + b), initialize w=0 and b=log(0.1/0.9), and mix features as (1-g)*full + g*recent. Shared linear dot-product scoring makes this equivalent to mixing candidate scores. Keep baseline dropout; avoid extra time features or text features in this first experiment. Recency here means interaction order, not elapsed time.\n\nCorrect masking: apply causal and padding masks in both encoders. PyTorch MultiheadAttention boolean True blocks attention. Prevent fully masked padded query rows with a harmless fallback key, then clear their outputs; also clear padded states after blocks. Chunk independently encoded windows to control memory, preserving one mean over all valid supervised positions. In evaluation mode, verify that changing interactions before the final five leaves recent-branch features and scores unchanged. Verify padded, short-history, and train-versus-predictor score consistency. A sliding window in every layer has receptive field 1+D*(w-1), so it is not a substitute for independent five-token windows.\n\nLoss and sampler: let N be the category's number of real items, excluding padding, and K=128. Require N>1. Use alpha=K/(N-1), beta=1-t+t*alpha, t=0.75. Draw negatives independently with replacement uniformly from all real item IDs, matching the official sampler, including possible target or known-positive collisions; never sample padding. This explicitly replaces the earlier proposed history exclusions. Excluding known training positives can be a separately labeled heuristic variant, but it invalidates direct transfer of the published calibration guarantee, and changing the denominator alone does not repair zero-probability exclusions. In float32 compute loss = mean_valid((beta*softplus(-positive_logit) + sum_j softplus(negative_logit_j))/(K+1)). Beta multiplies positive log-loss; do not use inverse-beta pos_weight, independently averaged positive/negative terms, or unstable sigmoid-power transformations. t=0 gives ordinary BCE with the same K-negative reduction, not equivalence to single-negative training. The sampler's possible false negatives are a limitation, not evidence that unobserved items are dislikes.\n\nEfficiency: avoid unnecessarily materializing B*L*K*D negative embeddings at once; chunk supervised positions or candidate scoring and preserve exact loss normalization. Profile actual step time and peak GPU memory; count profiling, diagnostics, debugging, failed runs and all four-category trials against the shared budget. Published 128-negative convergence times were about 1.81x on Steam and 1.32x on Gowalla, not transferable per-epoch or memory multipliers. Independent recent-window encoding adds further cost.\n\nSelection: predeclare four variants—baseline, gBCE-only, gate-only, combined—with checkpoint evaluation every 10 epochs, patience of five evaluations after the first evaluation, epoch ceiling 200 unless SASREC_EPOCHS overrides it, and earliest-checkpoint tie-breaking. Use one consistent checkpoint-selection rule based solely on unchanged four-category macro validation HR@10. Maintain an aggregate GPU-time ledger and reserve 10% of the total budget for diagnostics and final artifact checks; allocate the remaining time equally across variants and within each variant across categories. Budget exhaustion ends a run at its last completed checkpoint; record incomplete schedules and do not interpret them as matched convergence comparisons. Preserve category-level results and count all evaluated checkpoints as selection opportunities. If the unchanged evaluator exposes paired per-user validation hits, bootstrap users within each category with identical indices across models, average the four category differences, and report a 95% interval, category differences and discordant hits. One changed user hit is 0.5 percentage points within its category and 0.125 points in macro HR@10. These intervals condition on trained models and fixed candidate sets. Use Holm adjustment for planned variant-versus-baseline comparisons, while labeling post-selection inference exploratory; it does not remove adaptive selection optimism. Do not modify the evaluator to obtain unavailable outputs. Freeze selection before held-out evaluation.\n\nDiagnostics and serving: preserve DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), and diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis), returning measured metrics and definitions. view.model must remain the actual trained nn.Module. Expose mixed features through log2feats(sequence) and matching predict_score(features, items), retain item_emb, user_train, meta_data and review_data compatibility, and use view.model for architecture-dependent probes. Measure margins using actual mixed logits; mark unavailable category breakdowns unavailable rather than inventing metadata. Titles, descriptions, public histories and timestamps are the only permitted auxiliary information; review ratings/text, prices, stores and item categories are unavailable despite the generic input examples. Keep full-catalog unseen-item recommendations for the twenty diagnostic training users, five per category. Save gate parameters, window length, positional policy, model configuration, loss/sampler configuration and category routing with checkpoints. Update the predictor writer so working/predict.py reconstructs the same shared encoder and independent final-window branch.\n\nShortcut risks: the gate can collapse to one branch or encode history-length shortcuts, while uniform-negative training can reward item popularity. Track gate distributions by history length, recent-versus-full disagreement, and observed training-popularity strata alongside existing diagnostics. These are exploratory measurements, not new selection metrics or evidence of user satisfaction. Keep the selected architecture small and avoid fitting thresholds to the twenty simulator users.",
  "pseudocode": "K = 128\nfor variant in [baseline, gBCE_only, gate_only, combined]:\n    for category in FOUR_DATASETS:\n        reset_training_seed(42)\n        model = build_baseline_or_shared_window_model(variant)\n        N = number_of_real_items(category)\n        beta = 0.25 + 0.75 * K / (N - 1)\n        for epoch in predeclared_epoch_and_GPU_budget:\n            for _, sequence, positive, baseline_negative in train_loader:\n                valid = positive != 0\n                full = model.encode_full(sequence, causal=True)\n                if variant_has_gate:\n                    windows = gather_raw_left_padded_suffixes(sequence, valid, width=5)\n                    recent = encode_independently_in_chunks(windows, positions=range(5))[:, -1]\n                    h = full[valid]\n                    g = sigmoid(model.gate(concat(h, recent)))\n                    features = (1-g)*h + g*recent\n                else:\n                    features = full[valid]\n                p = model.predict_score(features, positive[valid]).float()\n                if variant_has_gBCE:\n                    negatives = uniform_with_replacement(real_item_ids, shape=(valid.sum(), K))\n                    n = model.predict_score(features, negatives).float()\n                    loss = ((beta*softplus(-p) + softplus(n).sum(-1))/(K+1)).mean()\n                else:\n                    loss = unchanged_baseline_BCE(features, positive[valid], baseline_negative[valid])\n                update_once(loss)\n            every_10_epochs_evaluate_unchanged_validation_protocol()\n            save_if_macro_HR10_improves_else_increment_patience()\n            stop_if_patience_or_epoch_or_GPU_limit_reached()\n        save_checkpoint_configuration_and_matching_predictor_writer()\nselect_variant_by_macro_validation_HR10_with_predeclared_ties()\nrun_measured_diagnostics_and_predictor_consistency_checks_within_reserved_budget()\nfreeze_selection_without_accessing_test_labels()",
  "originality": {
    "score": 4,
